{
  "version": "2.1.0",
  "$schema": "https://raw.githubusercontent.com/oasis-tcs/sarif-spec/main/sarif-2.1/schema/sarif-schema-2.1.0.json",
  "runs": [
    {
      "tool": {
        "driver": {
          "fullName": "Docker Scout",
          "informationUri": "https://docker.com/products/docker-scout",
          "name": "docker scout",
          "rules": [
            {
              "id": "CVE-2026-100351",
              "name": "OsPackageVulnerability",
              "shortDescription": {
                "text": "CVE-2026-100351: Authentication Bypass by Alternate Name"
              },
              "helpUri": "https://scout.docker.com/v/CVE-2026-100351?s=github&n=kin-openapi&ns=github.com%2Fgetkin&t=golang&vr=%3E%3D0.144.0%2C%3C0.147.0",
              "help": {
                "text": "## Summary\n\n`openapi3filter.ValidationHandler.Middleware` validates requests with kin-openapi's\nlegacy router, which selects an OpenAPI operation from the decoded `URL.Path`. The\nmethod is documented as implementing `gorilla/mux.MiddlewareFunc`, but a downstream\ngorilla/mux router configured with `UseEncodedPath()` selects its handler from the\nencoded representation (`URL.RawPath`).\n\nAn unauthenticated request such as `/public%2Fstatus` can therefore be validated as an\nintentionally public operation (`/public/status`) and then dispatched to a protected\noperation (`/{id}`). The configured, rejecting `AuthenticationFunc` is never invoked,\nand the protected downstream handler runs.\n\nThis is distinct from GHSA-r277-6w6q-xmqw. The behavior below uses an explicit\nauthentication callback that rejects missing credentials and remains reproducible\nafter the v0.144.0 fix for the unsafe default callback.\n\n## Affected products\n\n- Product: `github.com/getkin/kin-openapi`\n- Component: `openapi3filter.ValidationHandler.Middleware`\n- Confirmed post-GHSA affected releases: v0.144.0, v0.145.0, and v0.146.0\n- Reporter attestation: each listed release was checked out separately, the attached\n  single-file test was copied into `openapi3filter/`, and that test passed; maintainers\n  should independently confirm the version range\n- Confirmed affected development revision: `d33e44e67734dcde81b576582185e1343fb3985d`\n- v0.143.0 also reproduces this mechanism, but GHSA-r277-6w6q-xmqw already provides a\n  simpler bypass on that release; the distinct post-fix claim starts at v0.144.0\n- Earlier releases: not tested; please treat the introduction boundary as unknown\n\n## Security impact\n\nAn unauthenticated remote requester can reach a handler whose OpenAPI operation\nrequires authentication when all of the following are true:\n\n1. the application uses `ValidationHandler.Middleware` as the security gate;\n2. the downstream router preserves and interprets an encoded path, such as\n   gorilla/mux with `UseEncodedPath()`;\n3. a public exact route and a protected parameterized route overlap after decoding;\n4. the public and protected operations expose the same HTTP method;\n5. the protected handler relies on the validation layer's security decision.\n\nThe protected operation's path-parameter value is constrained: it is an encoded\nrepresentation that decodes to a public literal path (`public%2Fstatus` in this PoC),\nnot an arbitrary attacker-selected identifier. A protected operation without a\ncompatible parameterized path is not reachable through this specific mechanism.\n\n- Primitive: the security decision is made for a different OpenAPI operation than the\n  operation dispatched by the application router.\n- Demonstrated impact: a protected handler executes while the explicitly rejecting\n  `AuthenticationFunc` is never invoked.\n- Impact ceiling: confidentiality or integrity loss when the reachable protected\n  handler has that authority; the PoC does not demonstrate data disclosure or state\n  modification.\n- Availability impact: not demonstrated.\n\n## Root cause\n\nAt the confirmed development revision:\n\n- `openapi3filter/validation_handler.go`: `ValidationHandler.Load` constructs\n  `routers/legacy.NewRouter` unconditionally.\n- `routers/legacy/router.go`: route selection uses the decoded request path.\n- `routers/gorillamux/router.go`: the kin-openapi gorilla router constructs a\n  gorilla/mux router with `UseEncodedPath()`.\n- `ValidationHandler.Middleware` forwards the unchanged request after validation.\n\nThe component making the authentication decision and the component dispatching the\nhandler can therefore disagree about the selected operation.\n\n## Reproduction\n\nThe attached PoC uses a real `net/http` server, an explicit API-key callback that\nrejects missing credentials, and a downstream gorilla/mux router with\n`UseEncodedPath()`.\n\nFrom a kin-openapi checkout, copy the self-contained attachment\n`encoded_path_route_confusion_test.go.txt` into `openapi3filter/` as\n`encoded_path_route_confusion_test.go`, then run:\n\n```sh\ngo test -v ./openapi3filter -run '^TestEncodedPathAuthorizationBypass$' -count=1\n```\n\nExpected attack observation:\n\n```text\nstatus=202 downstream_route=/{id} protected_id=public%2Fstatus authentication_calls=0\nwiring=Router.Use status=202 downstream_route=/{id} protected_id=public%2Fstatus authentication_calls=0\n```\n\nControls included in the test show that:\n\n- `/ordinary` selects the protected operation, invokes authentication once, and is\n  rejected with HTTP 401;\n- `/public/status` reaches only the intentionally public handler;\n- the encoded request reaches the protected `/{id}` handler with HTTP 202 while the\n  authentication callback remains uncalled;\n- both external wrapping and the documented\n  `router.Use(validationHandler.Middleware)` wiring reproduce the same result.\n\n## Reverse-proxy reachability\n\nThe same request was reproduced through Nginx 1.31.3 using the official\n`nginx:alpine` image digest\n`sha256:4a73073bd557c65b759505da037898b61f1be6cbcc3c2c3aeac22d2a470c1752`.\n\nThe exact container invocation was:\n\n```sh\ndocker run --rm --name kin-openapi-nginx \\\n  --add-host=host.docker.internal:host-gateway \\\n  -p 18081:8080 -p 18082:8081 \\\n  -v \"$PWD/poc/nginx.conf:/etc/nginx/nginx.conf:ro\" \\\n  nginx:alpine\n```\n\nContainer port 8080 uses `proxy_pass http://host.docker.internal:18080;` without a\nreplacement URI. Host port 18081 therefore preserved `/public%2Fstatus`; the protected\nhandler returned HTTP 202 and the\nauthentication callback remained at zero calls. With\n`proxy_pass http://host.docker.internal:18080/;` on container port 8081 / host port\n18082, Nginx canonicalized the upstream URI; both layers selected `/public/status` and\nthe bypass did not occur.\n\nNginx access-log observations:\n\n```text\nserver_port=8080 \"GET /public%2Fstatus HTTP/1.1\" 202 0 \"curl/8.7.1\"\nserver_port=8081 \"GET /public%2Fstatus HTTP/1.1\" 204 0 \"curl/8.7.1\"\nserver_port=8080 \"GET /ordinary HTTP/1.1\" 401 0 \"curl/8.7.1\"\n```\n\nThe response route headers were `X-Poc-Route: /{id}` on the preserving listener and\n`X-Poc-Route: /public/status` on the canonicalizing listener. Backend observations for\nthe two requests were:\n\n```text\nrequest_uri=\"/public%2Fstatus\" path=\"/public/status\" raw_path=\"/public%2Fstatus\" auth_before=0\ncompleted request_uri=\"/public%2Fstatus\" auth_after=0\nrequest_uri=\"/public/status\" path=\"/public/status\" raw_path=\"\" auth_before=0\ncompleted request_uri=\"/public/status\" auth_after=0\n```\n\nThis control shows that upstream path handling is a deployment precondition rather\nthan claiming that every proxy configuration is affected.\n\nThe canonical protected control produced:\n\n```text\nrequest_uri=\"/ordinary\" path=\"/ordinary\" raw_path=\"\" auth_before=0\ncompleted request_uri=\"/ordinary\" auth_after=1\n```\n\n## Suggested remediation\n\nThe authentication layer and downstream dispatcher must select the same OpenAPI\noperation from the same request representation.\n\nPotential approaches:\n\n1. make `ValidationHandler` accept a caller-supplied `routers.Router` and require it to\n   match downstream routing semantics;\n2. document that `NewValidator` already accepts a router but is safe only when the\n   supplied router selects operations using the same semantics as the dispatcher;\n   callers must also supply an `AuthenticationFunc` through `ValidationOptions`, or\n   protected requests fail with the existing missing-authentication-service error;\n3. as an interim hardening option, make `ValidationHandler` fail closed on an encoded\n   path separator when `URL.Path` and `URL.RawPath` can select different operations,\n   and verify this behavior against the full suite;\n4. document the route-identity requirement and add encoded-separator regression tests.\n\nReplacing the legacy router with the kin-openapi gorilla router closes this PoC, but a\nlocal compatibility check changed two existing missing-path-parameter tests from HTTP\n400 semantics to route-not-found HTTP 404 semantics. A direct router swap is therefore\nnot a fully validated drop-in fix.\n\n## Public-reference triage\n\nThe public advisory list and related path-encoding reports reviewed on 2026-08-18 did\nnot describe this authentication-boundary crossing. Relevant nearby reports include\nissues #397, #1089, and #1105 and PR #400. This search is not proof of novelty; please\ndeduplicate against private reports.\n\n## Credit\n\nPreferred credit: Satoru Kanno (GMO CONNECT Inc., GMO Internet Group, Inc.)\n",
                "markdown": "> ## Summary\n\n`openapi3filter.ValidationHandler.Middleware` validates requests with kin-openapi's\nlegacy router, which selects an OpenAPI operation from the decoded `URL.Path`. The\nmethod is documented as implementing `gorilla/mux.MiddlewareFunc`, but a downstream\ngorilla/mux router configured with `UseEncodedPath()` selects its handler from the\nencoded representation (`URL.RawPath`).\n\nAn unauthenticated request such as `/public%2Fstatus` can therefore be validated as an\nintentionally public operation (`/public/status`) and then dispatched to a protected\noperation (`/{id}`). The configured, rejecting `AuthenticationFunc` is never invoked,\nand the protected downstream handler runs.\n\nThis is distinct from GHSA-r277-6w6q-xmqw. The behavior below uses an explicit\nauthentication callback that rejects missing credentials and remains reproducible\nafter the v0.144.0 fix for the unsafe default callback.\n\n## Affected products\n\n- Product: `github.com/getkin/kin-openapi`\n- Component: `openapi3filter.ValidationHandler.Middleware`\n- Confirmed post-GHSA affected releases: v0.144.0, v0.145.0, and v0.146.0\n- Reporter attestation: each listed release was checked out separately, the attached\n  single-file test was copied into `openapi3filter/`, and that test passed; maintainers\n  should independently confirm the version range\n- Confirmed affected development revision: `d33e44e67734dcde81b576582185e1343fb3985d`\n- v0.143.0 also reproduces this mechanism, but GHSA-r277-6w6q-xmqw already provides a\n  simpler bypass on that release; the distinct post-fix claim starts at v0.144.0\n- Earlier releases: not tested; please treat the introduction boundary as unknown\n\n## Security impact\n\nAn unauthenticated remote requester can reach a handler whose OpenAPI operation\nrequires authentication when all of the following are true:\n\n1. the application uses `ValidationHandler.Middleware` as the security gate;\n2. the downstream router preserves and interprets an encoded path, such as\n   gorilla/mux with `UseEncodedPath()`;\n3. a public exact route and a protected parameterized route overlap after decoding;\n4. the public and protected operations expose the same HTTP method;\n5. the protected handler relies on the validation layer's security decision.\n\nThe protected operation's path-parameter value is constrained: it is an encoded\nrepresentation that decodes to a public literal path (`public%2Fstatus` in this PoC),\nnot an arbitrary attacker-selected identifier. A protected operation without a\ncompatible parameterized path is not reachable through this specific mechanism.\n\n- Primitive: the security decision is made for a different OpenAPI operation than the\n  operation dispatched by the application router.\n- Demonstrated impact: a protected handler executes while the explicitly rejecting\n  `AuthenticationFunc` is never invoked.\n- Impact ceiling: confidentiality or integrity loss when the reachable protected\n  handler has that authority; the PoC does not demonstrate data disclosure or state\n  modification.\n- Availability impact: not demonstrated.\n\n## Root cause\n\nAt the confirmed development revision:\n\n- `openapi3filter/validation_handler.go`: `ValidationHandler.Load` constructs\n  `routers/legacy.NewRouter` unconditionally.\n- `routers/legacy/router.go`: route selection uses the decoded request path.\n- `routers/gorillamux/router.go`: the kin-openapi gorilla router constructs a\n  gorilla/mux router with `UseEncodedPath()`.\n- `ValidationHandler.Middleware` forwards the unchanged request after validation.\n\nThe component making the authentication decision and the component dispatching the\nhandler can therefore disagree about the selected operation.\n\n## Reproduction\n\nThe attached PoC uses a real `net/http` server, an explicit API-key callback that\nrejects missing credentials, and a downstream gorilla/mux router with\n`UseEncodedPath()`.\n\nFrom a kin-openapi checkout, copy the self-contained attachment\n`encoded_path_route_confusion_test.go.txt` into `openapi3filter/` as\n`encoded_path_route_confusion_test.go`, then run:\n\n```sh\ngo test -v ./openapi3filter -run '^TestEncodedPathAuthorizationBypass$' -count=1\n```\n\nExpected attack observation:\n\n```text\nstatus=202 downstream_route=/{id} protected_id=public%2Fstatus authentication_calls=0\nwiring=Router.Use status=202 downstream_route=/{id} protected_id=public%2Fstatus authentication_calls=0\n```\n\nControls included in the test show that:\n\n- `/ordinary` selects the protected operation, invokes authentication once, and is\n  rejected with HTTP 401;\n- `/public/status` reaches only the intentionally public handler;\n- the encoded request reaches the protected `/{id}` handler with HTTP 202 while the\n  authentication callback remains uncalled;\n- both external wrapping and the documented\n  `router.Use(validationHandler.Middleware)` wiring reproduce the same result.\n\n## Reverse-proxy reachability\n\nThe same request was reproduced through Nginx 1.31.3 using the official\n`nginx:alpine` image digest\n`sha256:4a73073bd557c65b759505da037898b61f1be6cbcc3c2c3aeac22d2a470c1752`.\n\nThe exact container invocation was:\n\n```sh\ndocker run --rm --name kin-openapi-nginx \\\n  --add-host=host.docker.internal:host-gateway \\\n  -p 18081:8080 -p 18082:8081 \\\n  -v \"$PWD/poc/nginx.conf:/etc/nginx/nginx.conf:ro\" \\\n  nginx:alpine\n```\n\nContainer port 8080 uses `proxy_pass http://host.docker.internal:18080;` without a\nreplacement URI. Host port 18081 therefore preserved `/public%2Fstatus`; the protected\nhandler returned HTTP 202 and the\nauthentication callback remained at zero calls. With\n`proxy_pass http://host.docker.internal:18080/;` on container port 8081 / host port\n18082, Nginx canonicalized the upstream URI; both layers selected `/public/status` and\nthe bypass did not occur.\n\nNginx access-log observations:\n\n```text\nserver_port=8080 \"GET /public%2Fstatus HTTP/1.1\" 202 0 \"curl/8.7.1\"\nserver_port=8081 \"GET /public%2Fstatus HTTP/1.1\" 204 0 \"curl/8.7.1\"\nserver_port=8080 \"GET /ordinary HTTP/1.1\" 401 0 \"curl/8.7.1\"\n```\n\nThe response route headers were `X-Poc-Route: /{id}` on the preserving listener and\n`X-Poc-Route: /public/status` on the canonicalizing listener. Backend observations for\nthe two requests were:\n\n```text\nrequest_uri=\"/public%2Fstatus\" path=\"/public/status\" raw_path=\"/public%2Fstatus\" auth_before=0\ncompleted request_uri=\"/public%2Fstatus\" auth_after=0\nrequest_uri=\"/public/status\" path=\"/public/status\" raw_path=\"\" auth_before=0\ncompleted request_uri=\"/public/status\" auth_after=0\n```\n\nThis control shows that upstream path handling is a deployment precondition rather\nthan claiming that every proxy configuration is affected.\n\nThe canonical protected control produced:\n\n```text\nrequest_uri=\"/ordinary\" path=\"/ordinary\" raw_path=\"\" auth_before=0\ncompleted request_uri=\"/ordinary\" auth_after=1\n```\n\n## Suggested remediation\n\nThe authentication layer and downstream dispatcher must select the same OpenAPI\noperation from the same request representation.\n\nPotential approaches:\n\n1. make `ValidationHandler` accept a caller-supplied `routers.Router` and require it to\n   match downstream routing semantics;\n2. document that `NewValidator` already accepts a router but is safe only when the\n   supplied router selects operations using the same semantics as the dispatcher;\n   callers must also supply an `AuthenticationFunc` through `ValidationOptions`, or\n   protected requests fail with the existing missing-authentication-service error;\n3. as an interim hardening option, make `ValidationHandler` fail closed on an encoded\n   path separator when `URL.Path` and `URL.RawPath` can select different operations,\n   and verify this behavior against the full suite;\n4. document the route-identity requirement and add encoded-separator regression tests.\n\nReplacing the legacy router with the kin-openapi gorilla router closes this PoC, but a\nlocal compatibility check changed two existing missing-path-parameter tests from HTTP\n400 semantics to route-not-found HTTP 404 semantics. A direct router swap is therefore\nnot a fully validated drop-in fix.\n\n## Public-reference triage\n\nThe public advisory list and related path-encoding reports reviewed on 2026-08-18 did\nnot describe this authentication-boundary crossing. Relevant nearby reports include\nissues #397, #1089, and #1105 and PR #400. This search is not proof of novelty; please\ndeduplicate against private reports.\n\n## Credit\n\nPreferred credit: Satoru Kanno (GMO CONNECT Inc., GMO Internet Group, Inc.)\n\n|                                                                                                                                 |\n|Package                                                                        pkg:golang/github.com/getkin/kin-openapi@0.144.0  |\n|Affected range                                                                 >=0.144.0,<0.147.0                                |\n|Fixed version                                                                  0.147.0                                           |\n|[[CVSS Score 7.4] [CVSS Vector CVSS:3.1/AV:N/AC:H/PR:N/UI:N/S:U/C:H/I:H/A:N]]                                                    |\n"
              },
              "properties": {
                "affected_version": ">=0.144.0,<0.147.0",
                "cvssV3": 7.4,
                "cvssV3_severity": "HIGH",
                "cvssV3_vector": "CVSS:3.1/AV:N/AC:H/PR:N/UI:N/S:U/C:H/I:H/A:N",
                "fixed_version": "0.147.0",
                "purls": [
                  "pkg:golang/github.com/getkin/kin-openapi@0.144.0"
                ],
                "security-severity": "7.4",
                "tags": [
                  "HIGH"
                ]
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "CVE-2026-100352",
              "name": "OsPackageVulnerability",
              "shortDescription": {
                "text": "CVE-2026-100352: Improper Check for Unusual or Exceptional Conditions"
              },
              "helpUri": "https://scout.docker.com/v/CVE-2026-100352?s=github&n=kin-openapi&ns=github.com%2Fgetkin&t=golang&vr=%3C0.148.0",
              "help": {
                "text": "## Summary\n\nkin-openapi guards against `NaN` and `Inf` values in its built-in schema visitor (`visitJSON`) by returning `ErrSchemaInputNaN` / `ErrSchemaInputInf` when a `float64` input violates those constraints. For OAS 3.1 specs, however, `Schema.VisitJSON` routes through `useJSONSchema2020`, which hands the value directly to `santhosh-tekuri/jsonschema/v6` v6.0.2 without applying the same guard.\n\nInside `jsonschema/v6` v6.0.2, `numValidate` builds a `*big.Rat` from the input via `big.Rat.SetString(fmt.Sprintf(\"%v\", v))`. For `NaN`/`Inf` this parse fails silently and returns `nil`. `numValidate` then dereferences the nil `*big.Rat` (in `math/big.(*Rat).Cmp`), producing a Go runtime panic.\n\nAn attacker delivers this by sending an HTTP request with `Content-Type: application/yaml` (or `application/x-yaml`) and a YAML body containing `.nan`, `.inf`, or `-.inf`. The `YamlBodyDecoder` decodes those tokens into `math.NaN()` / `math.Inf(±1)` (standard YAML 1.2 behavior), and they propagate to the unguarded `useJSONSchema2020` path whenever the schema declares a numeric constraint (`minimum`, `maximum`, `exclusiveMinimum`, `exclusiveMaximum`, or `multipleOf`).\n\n## Details\n### Root Cause\n\nThe built-in visitor `visitJSON` at `openapi3/schema.go:1968` guards NaN/Inf explicitly:\n\n```go\ncase float64:\n    if math.IsNaN(value) {\n        return ErrSchemaInputNaN\n    }\n    if math.IsInf(value, 0) {\n        return ErrSchemaInputInf\n    }\n```\n\nFor OAS 3.1 specs, `VisitJSON` dispatches to `useJSONSchema2020` (`openapi3/schema_jsonschema_validator.go:204`), which contains no such guard:\n\n```go\nfunc (schema *Schema) useJSONSchema2020(settings *schemaValidationSettings, value any) error {\n    validator, err := newJSONSchemaValidator(schema)\n    if err != nil {\n        return schema.visitJSON(settings, value)\n    }\n    return validator.validate(value)      // value handed straight through\n}\n```\n\n`jsonSchemaValidator.validate` calls `jsonschema/v6.Schema.Validate` (line 146), which reaches `numValidate` in v6.0.2. There, `big.Rat.SetString(fmt.Sprintf(\"%v\", v))` returns `(nil, false)` for `NaN`/`Inf`, and the code dereferences the `nil` `*big.Rat` in `Rat.Cmp`, causing the panic.\n\n## Proof of Concept\n\n### Environment\n\n- kin-openapi v0.144.0 (tag `v0.144.0`, commit `f0407d5`)\n- santhosh-tekuri/jsonschema/v6 v6.0.2 (transitive dep)\n- Go 1.26.7\n\n### Spec\n\nMinimal OAS 3.1 spec (`spec.yaml`) declaring a YAML body with a numeric constraint:\n\n```yaml\nopenapi: '3.1.0'\ninfo: { title: Test, version: '1.0' }\npaths:\n  /test:\n    post:\n      requestBody:\n        required: true\n        content:\n          application/yaml:\n            schema:\n              type: number\n              minimum: 0\n      responses:\n        '200': { description: OK }\n```\n\n### Trigger\n\nA single `POST /test` with `Content-Type: application/yaml` and body `.nan`, `.inf`, or `-.inf`. All three variants panic:\n\n```\n=== RUN   TestNaNInf\n[nan]    PANIC (confirms vuln): runtime error: invalid memory address or nil pointer dereference\n[inf]    PANIC (confirms vuln): runtime error: invalid memory address or nil pointer dereference\n[neginf] PANIC (confirms vuln): runtime error: invalid memory address or nil pointer dereference\n```\n\n### Panic stack trace (confirms exact chain)\n\n```\npanic: runtime error: invalid memory address or nil pointer dereference\n\nmath/big.(*Rat).Cmp(0x0, ...)                                       <- nil *big.Rat dereferenced\n    C:/Program Files/Go/src/math/big/rat.go:492\ngithub.com/santhosh-tekuri/jsonschema/v6.(*validator).numValidate(...)\n    .../jsonschema/v6@v6.0.2/validator.go:525\ngithub.com/santhosh-tekuri/jsonschema/v6.(*validator).validate(...)\n    .../jsonschema/v6@v6.0.2/validator.go:168\ngithub.com/santhosh-tekuri/jsonschema/v6.(*Schema).validate(...)\n    .../jsonschema/v6@v6.0.2/validator.go:34\ngithub.com/getkin/kin-openapi/openapi3.(*jsonSchemaValidator).validate(...)\n    openapi3/schema_jsonschema_validator.go:146\ngithub.com/getkin/kin-openapi/openapi3.(*Schema).useJSONSchema2020(...)   <- MISSING NaN/Inf guard\n    openapi3/schema_jsonschema_validator.go:211\ngithub.com/getkin/kin-openapi/openapi3.(*Schema).VisitJSON(...)\n    openapi3/schema.go:1946\ngithub.com/getkin/kin-openapi/openapi3filter.ValidateRequestBody(...)\n    openapi3filter/validate_request.go:364\ngithub.com/getkin/kin-openapi/openapi3filter.ValidateRequest(...)\n    openapi3filter/validate_request.go:96\n```\n\n### net/http behavior (severity nuance)\n\nBehind a standard `net/http` server, the panic is recovered per-connection. The client sees a dropped connection; the server process continues:\n\n```\nSending POST /test body=.nan against real net/http server...\n2026/08/25 http: panic serving 127.0.0.1:40625: runtime error: invalid memory address or nil pointer dereference\nCLIENT ERROR (connection dropped): Post \".../test\": EOF\nProcess still alive after request: true\n```\n\nThis is a **recoverable panic**, unlike the recursion finding. `recover()` catches it (verified by capturing the panic value in a `defer` in the same goroutine). Non-`net/http` hosts or validation performed in an application-spawned goroutine are not covered by that recovery and would experience a full-process crash.\n\n### Fix verification\n\nUpgrading only the transitive dependency to `jsonschema/v6@v6.0.3` (via `go get github.com/santhosh-tekuri/jsonschema/v6@v6.0.3`) eliminates the panic entirely; all three payloads return a clean validation error:\n\n```\n[nan]    Returned normally, err = request body has an error: doesn't match schema: validation failed due to: at '': invalid jsonType float64\n[inf]    Returned normally, err = request body has an error: doesn't match schema: validation failed due to: at '': invalid jsonType float64\n[neginf] Returned normally, err = request body has an error: doesn't match schema: validation failed due to: at '': invalid jsonType float64\n```\n\nThis confirms the upstream `jsonschema/v6` v6.0.3 release treats the NaN/Inf case as a bug and fixes it at the source. It also confirms `useJSONSchema2020` is the reachable path: patching only the transitive dep removes the panic without touching kin-openapi code.\n\n## Why This Is a Vulnerability, Not by Design\n\n- The library explicitly refuses NaN/Inf in its own visitor (`ErrSchemaInputNaN` / `ErrSchemaInputInf` at `schema.go:1968`). The stated policy is: these values are not allowed.\n- The OAS 3.1 dispatch (`useJSONSchema2020`) simply omits the same guard — a coding gap, not a design decision.\n- Upstream `jsonschema/v6` v6.0.3 fixes the same case as a bug. Two independent code owners agree it should not panic.\n- The trigger is a single HTTP request body an unauthenticated client can send. There is no legitimate content-authoring reason to submit `.nan` / `.inf` as a numeric field.\n\n## Impact\n\n- **Per-request denial of service (net/http hosts).** Every crafted request kills its own connection with zero attacker cost. Sustained flooding degrades service reliability and log noise.\n- **Full-process denial of service (non-net/http hosts or goroutine-spawned validation).** No `net/http` recovery wrapper is present; the whole process crashes on a single request.\n- **Attack surface is any OAS 3.1 API that accepts YAML bodies with numeric constraints.** These constraints (`minimum`, `maximum`, `exclusiveMinimum`, `exclusiveMaximum`, `multipleOf`) are common on any numeric field with a range.\n",
                "markdown": "> ## Summary\n\nkin-openapi guards against `NaN` and `Inf` values in its built-in schema visitor (`visitJSON`) by returning `ErrSchemaInputNaN` / `ErrSchemaInputInf` when a `float64` input violates those constraints. For OAS 3.1 specs, however, `Schema.VisitJSON` routes through `useJSONSchema2020`, which hands the value directly to `santhosh-tekuri/jsonschema/v6` v6.0.2 without applying the same guard.\n\nInside `jsonschema/v6` v6.0.2, `numValidate` builds a `*big.Rat` from the input via `big.Rat.SetString(fmt.Sprintf(\"%v\", v))`. For `NaN`/`Inf` this parse fails silently and returns `nil`. `numValidate` then dereferences the nil `*big.Rat` (in `math/big.(*Rat).Cmp`), producing a Go runtime panic.\n\nAn attacker delivers this by sending an HTTP request with `Content-Type: application/yaml` (or `application/x-yaml`) and a YAML body containing `.nan`, `.inf`, or `-.inf`. The `YamlBodyDecoder` decodes those tokens into `math.NaN()` / `math.Inf(±1)` (standard YAML 1.2 behavior), and they propagate to the unguarded `useJSONSchema2020` path whenever the schema declares a numeric constraint (`minimum`, `maximum`, `exclusiveMinimum`, `exclusiveMaximum`, or `multipleOf`).\n\n## Details\n### Root Cause\n\nThe built-in visitor `visitJSON` at `openapi3/schema.go:1968` guards NaN/Inf explicitly:\n\n```go\ncase float64:\n    if math.IsNaN(value) {\n        return ErrSchemaInputNaN\n    }\n    if math.IsInf(value, 0) {\n        return ErrSchemaInputInf\n    }\n```\n\nFor OAS 3.1 specs, `VisitJSON` dispatches to `useJSONSchema2020` (`openapi3/schema_jsonschema_validator.go:204`), which contains no such guard:\n\n```go\nfunc (schema *Schema) useJSONSchema2020(settings *schemaValidationSettings, value any) error {\n    validator, err := newJSONSchemaValidator(schema)\n    if err != nil {\n        return schema.visitJSON(settings, value)\n    }\n    return validator.validate(value)      // value handed straight through\n}\n```\n\n`jsonSchemaValidator.validate` calls `jsonschema/v6.Schema.Validate` (line 146), which reaches `numValidate` in v6.0.2. There, `big.Rat.SetString(fmt.Sprintf(\"%v\", v))` returns `(nil, false)` for `NaN`/`Inf`, and the code dereferences the `nil` `*big.Rat` in `Rat.Cmp`, causing the panic.\n\n## Proof of Concept\n\n### Environment\n\n- kin-openapi v0.144.0 (tag `v0.144.0`, commit `f0407d5`)\n- santhosh-tekuri/jsonschema/v6 v6.0.2 (transitive dep)\n- Go 1.26.7\n\n### Spec\n\nMinimal OAS 3.1 spec (`spec.yaml`) declaring a YAML body with a numeric constraint:\n\n```yaml\nopenapi: '3.1.0'\ninfo: { title: Test, version: '1.0' }\npaths:\n  /test:\n    post:\n      requestBody:\n        required: true\n        content:\n          application/yaml:\n            schema:\n              type: number\n              minimum: 0\n      responses:\n        '200': { description: OK }\n```\n\n### Trigger\n\nA single `POST /test` with `Content-Type: application/yaml` and body `.nan`, `.inf`, or `-.inf`. All three variants panic:\n\n```\n=== RUN   TestNaNInf\n[nan]    PANIC (confirms vuln): runtime error: invalid memory address or nil pointer dereference\n[inf]    PANIC (confirms vuln): runtime error: invalid memory address or nil pointer dereference\n[neginf] PANIC (confirms vuln): runtime error: invalid memory address or nil pointer dereference\n```\n\n### Panic stack trace (confirms exact chain)\n\n```\npanic: runtime error: invalid memory address or nil pointer dereference\n\nmath/big.(*Rat).Cmp(0x0, ...)                                       <- nil *big.Rat dereferenced\n    C:/Program Files/Go/src/math/big/rat.go:492\ngithub.com/santhosh-tekuri/jsonschema/v6.(*validator).numValidate(...)\n    .../jsonschema/v6@v6.0.2/validator.go:525\ngithub.com/santhosh-tekuri/jsonschema/v6.(*validator).validate(...)\n    .../jsonschema/v6@v6.0.2/validator.go:168\ngithub.com/santhosh-tekuri/jsonschema/v6.(*Schema).validate(...)\n    .../jsonschema/v6@v6.0.2/validator.go:34\ngithub.com/getkin/kin-openapi/openapi3.(*jsonSchemaValidator).validate(...)\n    openapi3/schema_jsonschema_validator.go:146\ngithub.com/getkin/kin-openapi/openapi3.(*Schema).useJSONSchema2020(...)   <- MISSING NaN/Inf guard\n    openapi3/schema_jsonschema_validator.go:211\ngithub.com/getkin/kin-openapi/openapi3.(*Schema).VisitJSON(...)\n    openapi3/schema.go:1946\ngithub.com/getkin/kin-openapi/openapi3filter.ValidateRequestBody(...)\n    openapi3filter/validate_request.go:364\ngithub.com/getkin/kin-openapi/openapi3filter.ValidateRequest(...)\n    openapi3filter/validate_request.go:96\n```\n\n### net/http behavior (severity nuance)\n\nBehind a standard `net/http` server, the panic is recovered per-connection. The client sees a dropped connection; the server process continues:\n\n```\nSending POST /test body=.nan against real net/http server...\n2026/08/25 http: panic serving 127.0.0.1:40625: runtime error: invalid memory address or nil pointer dereference\nCLIENT ERROR (connection dropped): Post \".../test\": EOF\nProcess still alive after request: true\n```\n\nThis is a **recoverable panic**, unlike the recursion finding. `recover()` catches it (verified by capturing the panic value in a `defer` in the same goroutine). Non-`net/http` hosts or validation performed in an application-spawned goroutine are not covered by that recovery and would experience a full-process crash.\n\n### Fix verification\n\nUpgrading only the transitive dependency to `jsonschema/v6@v6.0.3` (via `go get github.com/santhosh-tekuri/jsonschema/v6@v6.0.3`) eliminates the panic entirely; all three payloads return a clean validation error:\n\n```\n[nan]    Returned normally, err = request body has an error: doesn't match schema: validation failed due to: at '': invalid jsonType float64\n[inf]    Returned normally, err = request body has an error: doesn't match schema: validation failed due to: at '': invalid jsonType float64\n[neginf] Returned normally, err = request body has an error: doesn't match schema: validation failed due to: at '': invalid jsonType float64\n```\n\nThis confirms the upstream `jsonschema/v6` v6.0.3 release treats the NaN/Inf case as a bug and fixes it at the source. It also confirms `useJSONSchema2020` is the reachable path: patching only the transitive dep removes the panic without touching kin-openapi code.\n\n## Why This Is a Vulnerability, Not by Design\n\n- The library explicitly refuses NaN/Inf in its own visitor (`ErrSchemaInputNaN` / `ErrSchemaInputInf` at `schema.go:1968`). The stated policy is: these values are not allowed.\n- The OAS 3.1 dispatch (`useJSONSchema2020`) simply omits the same guard — a coding gap, not a design decision.\n- Upstream `jsonschema/v6` v6.0.3 fixes the same case as a bug. Two independent code owners agree it should not panic.\n- The trigger is a single HTTP request body an unauthenticated client can send. There is no legitimate content-authoring reason to submit `.nan` / `.inf` as a numeric field.\n\n## Impact\n\n- **Per-request denial of service (net/http hosts).** Every crafted request kills its own connection with zero attacker cost. Sustained flooding degrades service reliability and log noise.\n- **Full-process denial of service (non-net/http hosts or goroutine-spawned validation).** No `net/http` recovery wrapper is present; the whole process crashes on a single request.\n- **Attack surface is any OAS 3.1 API that accepts YAML bodies with numeric constraints.** These constraints (`minimum`, `maximum`, `exclusiveMinimum`, `exclusiveMaximum`, `multipleOf`) are common on any numeric field with a range.\n\n|                                                                                                                                 |\n|Package                                                                        pkg:golang/github.com/getkin/kin-openapi@0.144.0  |\n|Affected range                                                                 <0.148.0                                          |\n|Fixed version                                                                  0.148.0                                           |\n|[[CVSS Score 7.5] [CVSS Vector CVSS:3.1/AV:N/AC:L/PR:N/UI:N/S:U/C:N/I:N/A:H]]                                                    |\n"
              },
              "properties": {
                "affected_version": "<0.148.0",
                "cvssV3": 7.5,
